>> title: Corn and Carrot Pork Bone Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, corn, carrot, cantonese, old-fire soup, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 粟米紅蘿蔔豬骨湯, suk mai hung lo baak zyu gwat tong, suk mai hung lo bak jue gwat tong, corn and carrot pork bone soup, sweetcorn pork bone soup, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr
>> slack: forgiving — nothing in this pot turns and a fourth hour only makes the corn sweeter; the one real error is stripping the kernels off the cob, because the cob is where most of the sweetness comes out
>> step.1: The child's pot — sweet, mild, made all year, and the only dried thing in it is a fig. It is the one soup on this shelf that needs no explaining at the table.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the bones
>> step.3: rinse the figs
>> step.4: simmer 2 1/2 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.6: The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.

The child's pot — sweet, mild, made all year, and the only dried thing in it is a fig. It is the one soup on this shelf that needs no explaining at the table.

Blanch @pork neck bones{2%lb}(900 g) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{10%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse every bone under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Rinse @dried figs{2}(mou faa gwo; sweetens like the honey date but drier — 潤腸 is the word) under cold water.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched bones{} with @&(~1)figs{}, @sweet corn{2%ears}(cut into rounds, cob and all), @carrot{2%large}(in thick rounds), @ginger{2%slices} and @cold water{3%qt}(2.8 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{2 1/2%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
